# PART 11: The Decree of the Sun King - Sovereign Grace and Dueling Laws
# ==============================================================================
PARTS_11_TO_15[11] = [
    "When report of the triple duel at the Palais-Royal reached the royal cabinet of King Louis XIV at Versailles, the King's ministers and the Grand Inquisitors demanded the immediate enforcement of the royal edicts against dueling. Dueling between noblemen was considered a direct challenge to the sovereign authority of the Crown, punishable by severe exile or execution. Yet when the King heard the full details of the encounter, his reaction was entirely unexpected, characterized by royal amusement rather than monarchical wrath.",
    "Louis XIV listened with keen amusement as his marshals described how three armed officers had been cleanly disarmed by a single young woman in velvet breeches who had then casually returned to finish her dance. With a famous display of royal wit that would echo through French legal history, the Sun King smiled and declared that his royal edict against dueling applied strictly to men, remarking that the law said nothing about duels between a woman and men. With this sovereign quip, the King dismissed all charges, granting Julie her second royal pardon.",
    "The King's clemency solidified Julie's legendary status as an icon of individual liberty and fearless independence. Throughout the salons and coffee houses of Paris, she was celebrated as a living hero who had outsmarted both the finest swordsmen of the realm and the rigid legal structures of the absolute monarchy. Her portrait was painted by court artists, depicting her with a rapier in one hand and an operatic music score in the other, celebrated as the pride of France and a living wonder of the age.",
    "Despite the King's pardon, the wounded pride of the three defeated noblemen made a temporary absence from the capital prudent. Julie decided to embark on an international artistic tour, setting out northward toward the wealthy and cultured city of Brussels, the capital of the Spanish Netherlands, where grand opera was flourishing under princely patronage and enthusiastic audiences eager for French culture.",
    "The journey north through the flat, picturesque landscapes of Flanders was peaceful and reflective. Traveling in a comfortable private coach along canals lined with swaying poplars and historic windmills, Julie enjoyed the quiet beauty of the Low Countries. The crisp northern air and the gentle clatter of carriage wheels against brick-paved roads provided a restful interlude after the intense drama and social whirl of Paris.",
    "In every Flemish town along her route—from Lille to Tournai and Mons—Julie was greeted with honors by local theater enthusiasts and municipal dignitaries who had heard of her Parisian triumphs. She attended private musical receptions, conversing in fluent French and Italian, captivating her hosts with her refined intellect, gracious manners, and captivating singing that warmed the northern halls with southern passion.",
    "During the quiet evenings at roadside coaching inns, Julie spent hours reading philosophy and writing letters to her devoted friends in Paris, including Count d'Armagnac and Count d'Albert. Their letters, filled with warm affection and updates on Parisian artistic life, reassured her that her place in the hearts of the French public remained secure and beloved across the realm, awaiting her return.",
    "Julie found deep tranquility in the simple routines of travel. She would wake early to watch the sunrise over the misty Flemish meadows, listening to the distant chimes of carillon bells from medieval belfries. The slow, harmonious rhythm of the northern countryside brought a soothing sense of renewal to her spirit, washing away all stress, fatigue, and restlessness from her mind.",
    "She used the travel time to study new musical styles, listening to northern vocalists and Flemish organists in ancient Gothic cathedrals. She marveled at the rich polyphony and solemn acoustic majesty of the northern churches, incorporating these sacred harmonies into her own vocal artistry and broadening her musical horizons with profound artistic depth and spiritual resonance.",
    "As the grand spires and guildhalls of Brussels appeared on the horizon, glowing softly in the late afternoon sun, Julie looked forward to conquering a new royal court. Taking a slow, deep breath, she entered the gates of Brussels with calm assurance, ready to dazzle the international aristocracy with the peerless magic of her voice and the indomitable spirit of her soul."
]

